-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- the library's code of a definition, as a body
-- (part of machine-in-hyperactive: "the translation from the library's code of a definition to a
-- body"). The checker above checks bodies; a definition of the library is held as Code (lib/code.cub),
-- so the translation is what lets it check a definition that was written, not a body built by hand.
-- It is a table: each form of Code the kernel has a form for is that form, variables by the same
-- index; a let is its body with the bound term put in; a global is its own code as it was written
-- (def-source, the one way a definition's code is obtained: nk-src-prog uses it too); and every
-- form the kernel has no form for is nk-fail, which the checker refuses, so nothing untranslated
-- is ever accepted. A binder's multiplicity is not carried: the kernel has none.


